1484375, 0.0177001953125, 0.0333251953125, -0.003814697265625, 0.0126190185546875, -0.00318145751953125, 0.0458984375, -0.020355224609375, 0.005558013916015625, 0.013702392578125, 0.052734375, 0.03900146484375, 0.006908416748046875, -0.007190704345703125, 0.023895263671875, 0.00911712646484375, -0.0051116943359375, -0.0303955078125, 0.0208740234375, -0.0355224609375, 0.018707275390625, 0.0016269683837890625, 0.0268707275390625, 0.01194000244140625, -0.02484130859375, 0.02496337890625, -0.0311737060546875, -0.01611328125, -0.01058197021484375, 0.03466796875, -0.0031833648681640625, -0.004955291748046875, 0.0208587646484375, -0.034149169921875, 0.0177764892578125, -0.0183868408203125, 0.01470184326171875, -0.00800323486328125, 0.0125732421875, 0.0010738372802734375, -0.039459228515625, -0.008941650390625, -0.005741119384765625, 0.002956390380859375, -0.022979736328125, -0.00888824462890625, 0.013275146484375, 0.01348876953125, 0.01947021484375, -0.0210418701171875, 0.0023441314697265625, -0.01068115234375, 0.0089263916015625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**Introduction**\n\nThis document provides a beginner-friendly introduction to the basic rules and objectives of chess, a classic two-player board game renowned for its strategy and skill. Visual aids are highly recommended for learning; see the resources and diagrams referenced throughout.\n\n---\n\n**1. Chessboard and Piece Setup**\n\nChess is played on a square board with 64 squares arranged in an 8\u00d78 grid, alternating light and dark colors. Each player controls sixteen pieces: one king, one queen, two rooks, two bishops, two knights, and eight pawns. The board is set up so that each player has a white (or light) square on their right-hand corner.\n\n**Piece Placement:**\n- The second rank (row) from each player is filled with pawns.\n- The first rank (closest to each player), from left to right, is arranged as: rook, knight, bishop, queen, king, bishop, knight, rook.\n- The queen is placed on the square matching her color (white queen on a white square, black queen on a black square).\n- White's pieces occupy the first two ranks (rows 1 and 2), and Black's occupy the last two ranks (rows 7 and 8).\n\n*Tip: Visual diagrams are extremely helpful for setup. For a clear illustration, see [Chess.com\u2019s setup guide](https://www.chess.com/lessons/how-to-set-up-the-chessboard). Remember: \"queen on her color\" and \"white square on right.\"*\n\n---\n\n**2. Key Terms (Glossary)**\n\n- **Rank:** A horizontal row of squares (numbered 1 to 8).\n- **File:** A vertical column of squares (labeled a to h).\n- **Check:** The king is under immediate threat of capture.\n- **Checkmate:** The king is in check and cannot escape capture.\n- **Stalemate:** The player to move has no legal moves and is not in check.\n- **Capture:** Taking an opponent's piece by moving onto its square.\n- **Illegal Move:** A move not permitted by the rules (e.g., moving into check).\n- **Castling:** A special move involving the king and a rook, allowing both to move simultaneously under certain conditions.\n- **En Passant:** A special pawn capture that can occur immediately after an opponent\u2019s pawn moves two squares forward from its starting position.\n- **Promotion:** When a pawn reaches the last rank, it must be replaced with another piece (usually a queen).\n- **Algebraic Notation:** The standard system for recording chess moves using coordinates (e.g., e4, Nf3).\n\n---\n\n**3. How to Start a Game**\n\n- Set up the board as described above.\n- White always moves first, and players alternate turns.\n- On each turn, a player moves one piece according to its movement rules.\n\n*Tip: For a visual overview of the board and piece names, see [lichess.org\u2019s interactive board](https://lichess.org/learn#/).*\n\n---\n\n**4. Piece Movement**\n\nEach type of piece moves in a unique way. Only one piece may occupy a square at any time. Capturing is optional and occurs when a piece moves onto a square occupied by an opponent's piece, removing it from the board.\n\n- **King:** Moves one square in any direction (horizontally, vertically, or diagonally). The king cannot move into check (a threatened square).\n- **Queen:** Moves any number of squares in any direction (horizontally, vertically, or diagonally).\n- **Rook:** Moves any number of squares horizontally or vertically.\n- **Bishop:** Moves any number of squares diagonally.\n- **Knight:** Moves in an L-shape: two squares in one direction and then one square perpendicular. Knights can jump over other pieces.\n- **Pawn:** Moves forward one square (never backward). On its first move, a pawn may move forward two squares. Pawns capture diagonally forward one square. Pawns cannot move or capture backward. When a pawn reaches the last rank (the eighth rank for White, first rank for Black), it must be promoted immediately (see Special Moves).\n\n*Tip: For diagrams showing how each piece moves, visit [ChessKid\u2019s piece movement guide](https://www.chesskid.com/lessons/pieces).*\n\n---\n\n**5. Special Moves**\n\n- **Castling:** A special move involving the king and a rook. The king moves two squares toward a rook (either kingside or queenside), and the rook moves to the square next to the king, crossing over it. Castling can only occur if neither piece has moved, the squares between them are unoccupied, and the king is not in, moving through, or ending up in check.\n    - *Kingside castling (short):* King moves from e1 to g1 (White) or e8 to g8 (Black); rook moves to f1 (White) or f8 (Black).\n    - *Queenside castling (long):* King moves from e1 to c1 (White) or e8 to c8 (Black); rook moves to d1 (White) or d8 (Black).\n- **En Passant:** A special pawn capture. If an opposing pawn moves two squares forward from its starting position and lands beside your pawn, your pawn may capture it as if it had moved only one square. This capture must be made immediately on the next move, or the opportunity is lost. En passant is only possible right after the opponent's double-step pawn move.\n- **Promotion:** When a pawn reaches the farthest rank (the eighth rank for White, first rank for Black), it must be promoted immediately. The player chooses to replace the pawn with a queen, rook, bishop, or knight (but not a king), regardless of how many of each piece are already on the board. Most often, players choose a queen.\n\n*Tip: For visual explanations of special moves, see [Chess.com\u2019s special moves guide](https://www.chess.com/lessons/special-moves).*\n\n---\n\n**6. How to Win or Draw**\n\n**Winning the Game:**\n- The main objective is to checkmate the opponent's king.\n    - **Check:** A king is in check when it is under immediate threat of capture by an opponent's piece. The player must make a move to remove the check.\n    - **Checkmate:** Checkmate occurs when the king is in check and there is no legal move to escape the threat; the game ends immediately.\n\n**Draws (Ties):**\nA game can also end in a draw in several ways:\n- **Stalemate:** The player to move has no legal moves and is not in check.\n- **Insufficient material:** Neither player has enough pieces to force checkmate (e.g., only kings remain).\n- **Threefold repetition:** The same position occurs three times with the same player to move and the same possible moves. To claim this draw, a player must notify the arbiter (in formal play) or declare it to the opponent (in casual play) before making their move.\n- **Fifty-move rule:** Fifty consecutive moves have occurred without any pawn movement or piece capture. This draw must also be claimed by the player whose turn it is before making their move.\n- **Mutual agreement:** Both players agree to a draw at any time.\n\n*Note: \"Perpetual check\" is not a separate draw condition but often leads to threefold repetition or the fifty-move rule. For more on draw claims, see [FIDE Laws of Chess](https://handbook.fide.com/chapter/E012023).*\n\n---\n\n**7. Illegal Moves**\n\nIf a player makes an illegal move (such as moving into check or moving a piece in an invalid way), the move must be retracted and a legal move played instead.\n\n- **Casual Play:** Players usually correct the move and continue. No penalties are typically enforced.\n- **Formal/Tournament Play:** The opponent may claim an illegal move. Depending on the event\u2019s rules, the arbiter may add time to the opponent\u2019s clock or declare a loss after repeated offenses. In most tournaments, two illegal moves by the same player in a game result in a loss.\n\n*Tip: Always double-check your moves, especially in formal settings, to avoid penalties.*\n\n---\n\n**8. Chess Notation**\n\nChess is often recorded using algebraic notation, which labels files a\u2013h (left to right from White\u2019s perspective) and ranks 1\u20138 (nearest to farthest from White). Each square has a unique coordinate (e.g., e4, d5). Piece moves are written with the piece\u2019s initial (e.g., Nf3 for knight to f3), while pawn moves use only the destination square (e.g., e4).\n\n*Tip: For a beginner\u2019s guide to notation, see [Chess.com\u2019s notation tutorial](https://www.chess.com/terms/chess-notation).*\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nChess is a game of strategy, skill, and careful planning. By understanding the setup, movement of each piece, special moves, and the ways a game can end, beginners can start playing and enjoying chess. As you gain experience, you will discover deeper tactics and strategies that make chess endlessly fascinating.\n\n*Tip: Use online resources and interactive boards to practice and visualize the rules as you learn.*"}}